WEBVTT
TITLE Path Mtu Discovery And Effective Mss — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
A host on a 1500-byte link advertises 1460 bytes of data, because its own link can carry that.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
The path it actually uses has a 1400-byte hop, so the path MTU is 1400 and not 1500.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
1460 plus 40 bytes of header is 1500, and 1500 minus 1400 is 100 bytes too many.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The router cannot fragment a datagram with DF set, so it drops it and sends back the reason.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.500
The path MTU is the smallest link MTU on the way, and the first hop is only a starting guess.

6
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:45.000
One PPPoE hop takes the path to 1492, and the MSS that follows is 1452 over IPv4.

7
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:52.500
One tunnel hop takes the path to 1400, and the MSS that follows is 1360 over IPv4.

8
00:00:52.500 --> 00:01:00.000
A narrow IPv6 hop takes the path to 1280: the data left is 1220 over IPv6 and 1240 over IPv4.

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.500
The host starts from the first-hop MTU and sets the Don't Fragment bit on every datagram.

10
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:15.000
The 1400-byte hop cannot forward a 1500-byte datagram and cannot fragment it, so it drops it.

11
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:22.500
The answer is an ICMP type 3 code 4 message carrying the next-hop MTU, 1400.

12
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.000
The host lowers its estimate to 1400 and sends again; that datagram is forwarded and delivered.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.500
Two bytes of the ICMP header that used to be padding now carry the next-hop MTU.

14
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:45.000
The value is a datagram size: it counts the IP header and not the link headers.

15
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:52.500
So 1400 means 1400 including the 20-byte IP header, and the data is 1360.

16
00:01:52.500 --> 00:02:00.000
A zero in that field means an unmodified router, and the host must fall back to a search.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.500
Four paths, and the data each one leaves once the fixed headers are removed.

18
00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:15.000
The path sets a ceiling and the peer sets a promise; the sender uses the smaller of the two.

19
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.500
A peer offering 1300 on the 1400-byte path gives 1300; a peer offering 1460 gives 1360.

20
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:30.000
1300 bytes of data is 1340 on the wire and fits; 1460 is 1500 and does not.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.500
Without the field, the host has only the size it sent and a table of plausible MTUs.

22
00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:45.000
The table has eleven plateaus; 1492 stands for 1536, 1500 and 1492 together.

23
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:52.500
The greatest plateau below 1500 is 1492, still too big, so a second step is needed.

24
00:02:52.500 --> 00:03:00.000
Then 1006 fits, and the estimate sits 394 bytes and 28.14 per cent below the truth.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.500
On a Too Big message the host must reduce the estimate and stop provoking more of them.

26
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:15.000
It must never go below 68 octets, and never raise the estimate on a message's word.

27
00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:22.500
Raising waits five minutes after a Too Big message and one minute after a raise.

28
00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:30.000
The recommended values are ten minutes and two, and a cached estimate ages out at ten.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.500
IPv6 routers never fragment, so an oversized packet is dropped and answered.

30
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:45.000
The answer is ICMPv6 type 2, Packet Too Big, and there is no DF bit in IPv6 at all.

31
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Every IPv6 link must carry 1280 bytes, so a report below 1280 is discarded.

32
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
At the floor the data left is 1220 bytes over IPv6 and 1240 over IPv4.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
A firewall that drops ICMP errors leaves the sender with no answer at all.

34
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
The 60-byte handshake passes and the connection opens; the 1500-byte data does not.

35
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:22.500
The sender retransmits the same oversized segment: an open, silent, black-hole connection.

36
00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:30.000
Clamping the MSS to 1360, or probing from 1200 without ICMP, restores the flow.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:37.500
First the path MTU: the smallest hop MTU on the path, 1400 in this example.

38
00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:45.000
Then the MSS from the path: 1360 over IPv4, and 1220 at the IPv6 floor of 1280.

39
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:52.500
Then the smaller of that and the peer's promise: 1300 when the peer offers 1300.

40
00:04:52.500 --> 00:05:00.000
Then the probe, the floors of 68 and 1280, and the fallback when the answer is lost.

